1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,971
I tried to plan a seventy billion parameter model on one H

2
00:00:03,971 --> 00:00:04,867
one hundred.

3
00:00:04,867 --> 00:00:06,995
The weights are seventy gigabytes.

4
00:00:06,995 --> 00:00:08,339
The card has eighty.

5
00:00:08,339 --> 00:00:09,010
It fits,

6
00:00:09,010 --> 00:00:10,298
with room to spare.

7
00:00:10,648 --> 00:00:13,432
Then I added thirty-two people chatting at once,

8
00:00:13,432 --> 00:00:17,448
and the memory needed jumped to one hundred and eighty-seven gigabytes.

9
00:00:17,448 --> 00:00:18,465
Not even close.

10
00:00:18,465 --> 00:00:20,500
In this lesson you will learn why,

11
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:23,123
and you will be able to do this sum yourself.

12
00:00:24,373 --> 00:00:27,776
Welcome to lesson one of the cloud for A I engineers course,

13
00:00:27,776 --> 00:00:29,104
on The Code Notebook.

14
00:00:29,454 --> 00:00:32,589
The handwritten notes and the code for this lesson are linked in

15
00:00:32,589 --> 00:00:33,511
the description.

16
00:00:34,761 --> 00:00:37,959
This is a twelve lesson course on the cloud side of A

17
00:00:37,959 --> 00:00:38,969
I engineering.

18
00:00:38,969 --> 00:00:41,046
We start with the chips you rent.

19
00:00:41,046 --> 00:00:42,168
Then containers,

20
00:00:42,168 --> 00:00:43,234
data, training,

21
00:00:43,234 --> 00:00:44,469
serving, shipping,

22
00:00:44,469 --> 00:00:45,759
security, and cost.

23
00:00:45,759 --> 00:00:48,397
The last lesson is a learning path for you.

24
00:00:48,747 --> 00:00:50,583
One project runs through it:

25
00:00:50,583 --> 00:00:51,788
ShopNest Copilot,

26
00:00:51,788 --> 00:00:54,600
an A I support assistant for an online store,

27
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:57,126
which we move to the cloud step by step.

28
00:00:57,126 --> 00:00:59,019
And one rule runs through it.

29
00:00:59,019 --> 00:01:01,659
A number I measured is marked as measured.

30
00:01:01,659 --> 00:01:04,357
A number from a vendor is marked as theirs,

31
00:01:04,357 --> 00:01:05,160
and dated.

32
00:01:06,410 --> 00:01:08,744
First, what does the cloud mean here?

33
00:01:08,744 --> 00:01:12,045
It means renting someone else's computers by the hour.

34
00:01:12,045 --> 00:01:14,663
For A I that means renting graphics cards,

35
00:01:14,663 --> 00:01:15,574
called GPUs,

36
00:01:15,574 --> 00:01:18,477
which cost too much to buy and are hard to get.

37
00:01:18,827 --> 00:01:22,694
Think of a restaurant that rents a kitchen instead of building one.

38
00:01:22,694 --> 00:01:24,491
You pay for the time you use.

39
00:01:24,491 --> 00:01:27,160
The skill is knowing which equipment to rent,

40
00:01:27,160 --> 00:01:30,646
and that starts with understanding what a GPU actually does.

41
00:01:31,896 --> 00:01:33,430
Your laptop has a C P U.

42
00:01:33,430 --> 00:01:35,183
It has a few strong workers,

43
00:01:35,183 --> 00:01:37,813
and each one is good at many different jobs.

44
00:01:38,163 --> 00:01:40,780
A GPU has thousands of simple workers.

45
00:01:40,780 --> 00:01:43,147
Each can only do basic arithmetic,

46
00:01:43,147 --> 00:01:45,950
but all of them do it at the same moment.

47
00:01:46,300 --> 00:01:49,152
Running an A I model is mostly one job,

48
00:01:49,152 --> 00:01:51,739
multiplying huge tables of numbers.

49
00:01:51,739 --> 00:01:56,051
That job splits perfectly across thousands of simple workers.

50
00:01:56,051 --> 00:01:59,102
That is the whole reason A I runs on GPUs.

51
00:02:00,352 --> 00:02:02,892
A GPU spec sheet is a wall of numbers,

52
00:02:02,892 --> 00:02:05,976
but only four of them decide almost everything.

53
00:02:05,976 --> 00:02:07,246
One, memory size,

54
00:02:07,246 --> 00:02:09,060
which tells you what fits.

55
00:02:09,060 --> 00:02:10,390
Two, memory speed,

56
00:02:10,390 --> 00:02:13,474
which tells you how fast a chat model can talk.

57
00:02:13,824 --> 00:02:14,875
Three, compute,

58
00:02:14,875 --> 00:02:18,193
which tells you how fast it can think through a big job.

59
00:02:18,193 --> 00:02:18,912
And four,

60
00:02:18,912 --> 00:02:20,681
the connection between GPUs,

61
00:02:20,681 --> 00:02:23,612
which tells you how far you can spread one model.

62
00:02:23,612 --> 00:02:25,326
We take them one at a time.

63
00:02:26,576 --> 00:02:27,438
Number one,

64
00:02:27,438 --> 00:02:28,358
memory size.

65
00:02:28,358 --> 00:02:31,576
To use it we need to know what goes into that memory

66
00:02:31,576 --> 00:02:34,219
first. A model is a giant list of numbers.

67
00:02:34,219 --> 00:02:36,403
Each number is called a parameter,

68
00:02:36,403 --> 00:02:37,323
or a weight.

69
00:02:37,323 --> 00:02:41,115
A seventy billion parameter model has seventy billion of them.

70
00:02:41,465 --> 00:02:45,286
Training is the process of finding good values for those numbers.

71
00:02:45,286 --> 00:02:46,393
Using the model,

72
00:02:46,393 --> 00:02:47,556
called inference,

73
00:02:47,556 --> 00:02:51,598
means loading all of them into memory and doing arithmetic with them.

74
00:02:51,598 --> 00:02:53,592
So the first question is always:

75
00:02:53,592 --> 00:02:55,696
how much room do the numbers take?

76
00:02:56,946 --> 00:02:58,061
Here is the sum,

77
00:02:58,061 --> 00:02:59,957
and it is just multiplication.

78
00:02:59,957 --> 00:03:04,084
Memory for the weights equals the number of parameters times the bytes

79
00:03:04,084 --> 00:03:05,311
each number takes.

80
00:03:05,311 --> 00:03:06,983
In the most common format,

81
00:03:06,983 --> 00:03:08,768
each number takes two bytes.

82
00:03:09,118 --> 00:03:12,126
So an eight billion model needs sixteen gigabytes.

83
00:03:12,126 --> 00:03:14,801
Seventy billion needs one hundred and forty.

84
00:03:14,801 --> 00:03:18,255
Four hundred and five billion needs eight hundred and ten.

85
00:03:18,255 --> 00:03:19,536
This is arithmetic,

86
00:03:19,536 --> 00:03:21,932
and you can check it with a calculator.

87
00:03:23,182 --> 00:03:24,188
Why two bytes?

88
00:03:24,188 --> 00:03:27,487
Because each number is stored with a certain precision.

89
00:03:27,487 --> 00:03:29,108
Think of writing a price.

90
00:03:29,108 --> 00:03:30,729
You can write two pounds,

91
00:03:30,729 --> 00:03:31,903
forty-nine pence,

92
00:03:31,903 --> 00:03:33,413
or round to two pounds.

93
00:03:33,413 --> 00:03:36,544
Rounding takes less space and loses a little detail.

94
00:03:36,894 --> 00:03:39,479
The common formats are called B F sixteen,

95
00:03:39,479 --> 00:03:40,996
F P eight and F P four.

96
00:03:40,996 --> 00:03:42,288
They use two bytes,

97
00:03:42,288 --> 00:03:43,019
one byte,

98
00:03:43,019 --> 00:03:44,761
and half a byte per number.

99
00:03:44,761 --> 00:03:47,009
Halving the bytes halves the memory.

100
00:03:47,009 --> 00:03:50,437
Newer chips can do arithmetic in the smaller formats too.

101
00:03:51,687 --> 00:03:54,861
Now line the weights up against real GPU memory.

102
00:03:54,861 --> 00:03:57,791
These sizes come from vendor specifications,

103
00:03:57,791 --> 00:03:59,988
as of October twenty twenty-six.

104
00:03:59,988 --> 00:04:02,552
An H one hundred has eighty gigabytes.

105
00:04:02,552 --> 00:04:05,665
An H two hundred has one hundred and forty-one.

106
00:04:05,665 --> 00:04:08,778
A B two hundred has one hundred and ninety-two.

107
00:04:08,778 --> 00:04:12,135
A B three hundred has two hundred and eighty-eight.

108
00:04:12,485 --> 00:04:15,726
Seventy billion in F P eight is seventy gigabytes.

109
00:04:15,726 --> 00:04:17,646
It fits on an H one hundred,

110
00:04:17,646 --> 00:04:18,726
but only just.

111
00:04:19,076 --> 00:04:22,531
Seventy billion in B F sixteen is one hundred and forty.

112
00:04:22,531 --> 00:04:25,180
That does not fit an H one hundred at all.

113
00:04:25,180 --> 00:04:26,849
You would need two cards,

114
00:04:26,849 --> 00:04:28,001
or a bigger one.

115
00:04:29,251 --> 00:04:32,662
A useful way to remember the chips is three memory tiers.

116
00:04:32,662 --> 00:04:35,514
Around eighty gigabytes is the older workhorse.

117
00:04:35,514 --> 00:04:39,484
Between one hundred and forty and one hundred and ninety-two is the

118
00:04:39,484 --> 00:04:40,379
middle tier.

119
00:04:40,379 --> 00:04:44,070
And two hundred and eighty-eight gigabytes is the newest tier.

120
00:04:44,420 --> 00:04:46,045
Across these generations,

121
00:04:46,045 --> 00:04:50,080
memory per card has gone from eighty to two hundred and eighty-eight

122
00:04:50,080 --> 00:04:52,770
gigabytes. That matters more than it sounds,

123
00:04:52,770 --> 00:04:56,805
because memory decides whether your model needs one card or several.

124
00:04:58,055 --> 00:05:00,289
But weights are only half the story,

125
00:05:00,289 --> 00:05:02,691
and this is the part that surprised me.

126
00:05:02,691 --> 00:05:04,478
When a model writes a reply,

127
00:05:04,478 --> 00:05:08,555
it does not re-read the whole conversation from scratch for every new

128
00:05:08,555 --> 00:05:09,952
word. It keeps notes.

129
00:05:10,302 --> 00:05:13,412
Think of a waiter with a notepad for each table.

130
00:05:13,412 --> 00:05:15,386
The notepad is the K V cache.

131
00:05:15,386 --> 00:05:18,736
Every token in every conversation adds a line to it,

132
00:05:18,736 --> 00:05:22,265
and the notes must stay in GPU memory while the chat is

133
00:05:22,265 --> 00:05:22,803
open.

134
00:05:24,053 --> 00:05:25,642
How big are those notes?

135
00:05:25,642 --> 00:05:26,948
Here is the recipe.

136
00:05:26,948 --> 00:05:29,388
Two, because we keep a key and a value.

137
00:05:29,388 --> 00:05:31,886
Times the number of layers in the model.

138
00:05:31,886 --> 00:05:33,815
Times the number of K V heads.

139
00:05:34,165 --> 00:05:36,307
Times how many numbers each head holds.

140
00:05:36,307 --> 00:05:37,851
Times the bytes per number.

141
00:05:38,201 --> 00:05:40,272
For a seventy billion class model,

142
00:05:40,272 --> 00:05:43,270
and I am assuming a typical shape of eighty layers,

143
00:05:43,270 --> 00:05:46,377
eight heads and one hundred and twenty-eight numbers,

144
00:05:46,377 --> 00:05:49,212
that is three hundred and twenty-seven thousand,

145
00:05:49,212 --> 00:05:51,011
six hundred and eighty bytes.

146
00:05:51,011 --> 00:05:52,482
For every single token.

147
00:05:53,732 --> 00:05:55,390
Now go back to the opening.

148
00:05:55,390 --> 00:05:57,529
Start with the weights in F P eight.

149
00:05:57,529 --> 00:05:58,705
Seventy gigabytes.

150
00:05:59,055 --> 00:06:00,682
Now add thirty-two chats,

151
00:06:00,682 --> 00:06:04,833
each with eight thousand one hundred and ninety-two tokens of history.

152
00:06:04,833 --> 00:06:08,254
At three hundred and twenty-seven thousand bytes a token,

153
00:06:08,254 --> 00:06:10,610
that is eighty-six gigabytes of notes.

154
00:06:10,610 --> 00:06:12,685
More than the weights themselves.

155
00:06:13,035 --> 00:06:15,717
Add twenty percent headroom for everything else,

156
00:06:15,717 --> 00:06:18,811
and the total is one hundred and eighty-seven gigabytes.

157
00:06:19,161 --> 00:06:20,913
An H one hundred fails.

158
00:06:20,913 --> 00:06:22,664
An H two hundred fails.

159
00:06:22,664 --> 00:06:24,610
A B two hundred just fits.

160
00:06:24,610 --> 00:06:27,593
A B three hundred fits with room to spare.

161
00:06:28,843 --> 00:06:30,877
Let me show the per chat numbers.

162
00:06:30,877 --> 00:06:34,890
One chat of eight thousand tokens costs two point six eight gigabytes

163
00:06:34,890 --> 00:06:36,760
of notes in the common format,

164
00:06:36,760 --> 00:06:39,343
or one point three four in the smaller one.

165
00:06:39,693 --> 00:06:42,282
Thirty-two chats cost eighty-five point nine,

166
00:06:42,282 --> 00:06:43,761
or forty-two point nine.

167
00:06:43,761 --> 00:06:47,035
Remember these are arithmetic with an assumed model shape.

168
00:06:47,035 --> 00:06:48,567
They are not a benchmark,

169
00:06:48,567 --> 00:06:50,574
but they show the pattern clearly.

170
00:06:51,824 --> 00:06:54,682
So the rule for fitting a model is three parts,

171
00:06:54,682 --> 00:06:55,354
not one.

172
00:06:55,354 --> 00:06:56,251
The weights,

173
00:06:56,251 --> 00:06:59,388
plus the notes for every chat you want open at once,

174
00:06:59,388 --> 00:07:00,397
plus headroom.

175
00:07:00,397 --> 00:07:03,534
Leave twenty to forty percent for the working space,

176
00:07:03,534 --> 00:07:04,767
the fragmentation,

177
00:07:04,767 --> 00:07:07,288
and the extra buffers the software needs.

178
00:07:07,638 --> 00:07:10,407
If you remember only one thing from this lesson,

179
00:07:10,407 --> 00:07:12,802
remember that the question is never just,

180
00:07:12,802 --> 00:07:14,027
does the model fit.

181
00:07:14,027 --> 00:07:14,559
It is,

182
00:07:14,559 --> 00:07:16,849
do the model and its conversations fit.

183
00:07:18,099 --> 00:07:19,739
You can shrink the notes.

184
00:07:19,739 --> 00:07:23,927
Store them in the smaller one byte format and the eighty-six gigabytes

185
00:07:23,927 --> 00:07:25,285
becomes forty-three,

186
00:07:25,285 --> 00:07:26,643
which my sum showed.

187
00:07:26,643 --> 00:07:29,981
Modern models also share notes between attention heads,

188
00:07:29,981 --> 00:07:31,962
which is why I used only eight.

189
00:07:32,312 --> 00:07:35,714
And serving software can hand out notes in small pages,

190
00:07:35,714 --> 00:07:37,328
so no chat wastes space.

191
00:07:37,328 --> 00:07:40,212
I am teaching those last two as concepts here.

192
00:07:40,212 --> 00:07:42,576
They are not measured in this lesson.

193
00:07:42,576 --> 00:07:44,133
Lesson six goes deeper.

194
00:07:45,383 --> 00:07:46,195
Number two,

195
00:07:46,195 --> 00:07:47,116
memory speed.

196
00:07:47,116 --> 00:07:50,474
Imagine a chef who must walk to the fridge for every dish.

197
00:07:50,474 --> 00:07:53,074
It does not matter how fast their hands are.

198
00:07:53,074 --> 00:07:54,699
The walking sets the pace.

199
00:07:55,049 --> 00:07:57,401
Writing a chat reply works like this.

200
00:07:57,401 --> 00:07:58,892
For every single word,

201
00:07:58,892 --> 00:08:02,276
the GPU must fetch the model's weights from its memory.

202
00:08:02,276 --> 00:08:03,825
The speed of that walk,

203
00:08:03,825 --> 00:08:06,693
measured in gigabytes or terabytes per second,

204
00:08:06,693 --> 00:08:08,070
is called bandwidth.

205
00:08:09,320 --> 00:08:11,335
Here is the rule that follows.

206
00:08:11,335 --> 00:08:12,876
To make one new token,

207
00:08:12,876 --> 00:08:15,187
the GPU reads all the weights once.

208
00:08:15,187 --> 00:08:18,980
So the fastest it can possibly go is the weight size divided

209
00:08:18,980 --> 00:08:20,402
by the memory speed.

210
00:08:20,752 --> 00:08:22,391
That is a floor on time,

211
00:08:22,391 --> 00:08:23,444
not a promise.

212
00:08:23,444 --> 00:08:25,083
Real systems are slower.

213
00:08:25,083 --> 00:08:27,073
But it explains something odd:

214
00:08:27,073 --> 00:08:30,526
giving a chat model more arithmetic power barely helps.

215
00:08:30,526 --> 00:08:32,866
Giving it faster memory helps a lot.

216
00:08:34,116 --> 00:08:36,709
Let me test that on a machine I have.

217
00:08:36,709 --> 00:08:38,037
This is my C P U,

218
00:08:38,037 --> 00:08:38,922
not a GPU,

219
00:08:38,922 --> 00:08:40,946
so the speeds are far lower.

220
00:08:40,946 --> 00:08:42,716
But the law is the same.

221
00:08:42,716 --> 00:08:45,751
I multiply a table of numbers by one vector.

222
00:08:45,751 --> 00:08:48,660
That is exactly one token's worth of work.

223
00:08:49,010 --> 00:08:51,523
I measure the time for four table sizes.

224
00:08:51,523 --> 00:08:52,265
One core,

225
00:08:52,265 --> 00:08:53,636
plain float numbers,

226
00:08:53,636 --> 00:08:55,006
best of seven tries.

227
00:08:55,006 --> 00:08:56,491
The machine is shared,

228
00:08:56,491 --> 00:08:58,490
so I ran the whole thing twice,

229
00:08:58,490 --> 00:09:00,032
and the results agreed.

230
00:09:01,282 --> 00:09:04,930
A four megabyte table takes point one five milliseconds.

231
00:09:04,930 --> 00:09:07,605
Seventeen megabytes takes point six two.

232
00:09:07,605 --> 00:09:10,827
Sixty-seven megabytes takes two point four three.

233
00:09:11,177 --> 00:09:15,174
Then two hundred and sixty-eight megabytes takes twenty point three five

234
00:09:15,174 --> 00:09:16,069
milliseconds.

235
00:09:16,419 --> 00:09:20,095
Four times the bytes took eight point four times the time.

236
00:09:20,095 --> 00:09:21,399
That surprised me.

237
00:09:21,399 --> 00:09:24,601
The small tables fit inside the chip's fast cache.

238
00:09:24,601 --> 00:09:27,269
The big one had to come from main memory,

239
00:09:27,269 --> 00:09:29,284
which was about twice as slow.

240
00:09:29,284 --> 00:09:33,435
That is exactly the problem GPUs solve with very fast memory right

241
00:09:33,435 --> 00:09:34,680
next to the chip.

242
00:09:35,930 --> 00:09:38,249
Now use the same law on real GPUs,

243
00:09:38,249 --> 00:09:40,447
with the vendors' memory speeds.

244
00:09:40,447 --> 00:09:42,461
Seventy gigabytes of weights,

245
00:09:42,461 --> 00:09:44,231
read once for each token.

246
00:09:44,231 --> 00:09:45,696
On an H one hundred,

247
00:09:45,696 --> 00:09:48,443
at three point four terabytes per second,

248
00:09:48,443 --> 00:09:51,312
the floor is twenty point six milliseconds.

249
00:09:51,312 --> 00:09:54,180
About forty-nine tokens per second at most.

250
00:09:54,530 --> 00:09:55,705
An H two hundred,

251
00:09:55,705 --> 00:09:58,221
at four point eight terabytes per second,

252
00:09:58,221 --> 00:10:00,569
gives fourteen point six milliseconds.

253
00:10:00,569 --> 00:10:01,687
A B two hundred,

254
00:10:01,687 --> 00:10:02,414
at eight,

255
00:10:02,414 --> 00:10:03,980
gives eight point eight.

256
00:10:04,330 --> 00:10:05,312
Same weights,

257
00:10:05,312 --> 00:10:06,467
same arithmetic.

258
00:10:06,467 --> 00:10:09,065
The only change is the speed of the walk.

259
00:10:09,065 --> 00:10:10,047
And remember,

260
00:10:10,047 --> 00:10:12,530
this is arithmetic from specifications,

261
00:10:12,530 --> 00:10:14,378
a floor and not a benchmark.

262
00:10:15,628 --> 00:10:18,676
Memory speed has roughly doubled every generation.

263
00:10:18,676 --> 00:10:22,570
About three point four terabytes per second on the H one hundred,

264
00:10:22,570 --> 00:10:24,941
four point eight on the H two hundred,

265
00:10:24,941 --> 00:10:26,803
eight on the Blackwell chips,

266
00:10:26,803 --> 00:10:30,133
and around twenty-two promised for the next generation,

267
00:10:30,133 --> 00:10:32,560
which is still in limited availability.

268
00:10:32,910 --> 00:10:34,632
Notice the H two hundred.

269
00:10:34,632 --> 00:10:37,721
It has the same arithmetic as the H one hundred,

270
00:10:37,721 --> 00:10:39,622
only more and faster memory.

271
00:10:39,622 --> 00:10:40,394
For chat,

272
00:10:40,394 --> 00:10:42,591
that alone makes it a better buy.

273
00:10:43,841 --> 00:10:44,753
Number three,

274
00:10:44,753 --> 00:10:47,488
compute. That is the speed of the cooks' hands,

275
00:10:47,488 --> 00:10:48,615
counted in flops,

276
00:10:48,615 --> 00:10:51,886
which is simply multiplications and additions per second.

277
00:10:51,886 --> 00:10:55,480
Compute matters when there is a lot of arithmetic for each byte

278
00:10:55,480 --> 00:10:55,962
read.

279
00:10:56,312 --> 00:10:58,970
Reading your long prompt is that kind of job,

280
00:10:58,970 --> 00:11:01,628
because all the words are processed together.

281
00:11:01,628 --> 00:11:02,713
Training is too.

282
00:11:02,713 --> 00:11:03,635
A chat reply,

283
00:11:03,635 --> 00:11:04,883
one word at a time,

284
00:11:04,883 --> 00:11:05,968
is the opposite.

285
00:11:05,968 --> 00:11:09,060
So the same chip can be limited by different numbers,

286
00:11:09,060 --> 00:11:10,416
depending on the job.

287
00:11:11,666 --> 00:11:14,312
So how do providers make chat affordable?

288
00:11:14,312 --> 00:11:16,371
By serving many people at once.

289
00:11:16,371 --> 00:11:19,135
If the GPU is walking to the fridge anyway,

290
00:11:19,135 --> 00:11:22,488
it can carry orders for many tables on the same trip.

291
00:11:22,838 --> 00:11:24,098
I measured this.

292
00:11:24,098 --> 00:11:26,998
One token at a time on my C P U gave about

293
00:11:26,998 --> 00:11:29,960
three hundred and ninety tokens per second.

294
00:11:29,960 --> 00:11:33,175
Sixty-four at a time gave about three thousand.

295
00:11:33,525 --> 00:11:37,223
Two hundred and fifty-six at a time gave about four thousand.

296
00:11:37,223 --> 00:11:40,238
That is roughly ten times more tokens per second,

297
00:11:40,238 --> 00:11:43,424
while each step took about twenty-four times longer.

298
00:11:43,424 --> 00:11:45,984
Reading the weights once served everyone.

299
00:11:47,234 --> 00:11:49,467
Why not batch a thousand people then?

300
00:11:49,467 --> 00:11:51,917
Because every person needs their notepad.

301
00:11:51,917 --> 00:11:54,149
Each extra chat adds notes to memory,

302
00:11:54,149 --> 00:11:56,436
which is the thing we just calculated.

303
00:11:56,786 --> 00:12:00,208
Which means memory size and memory speed are linked.

304
00:12:00,208 --> 00:12:03,875
Memory size limits how many chats you can serve at once.

305
00:12:03,875 --> 00:12:06,809
Memory speed limits how fast each step runs.

306
00:12:06,809 --> 00:12:09,192
A good serving setup balances both.

307
00:12:10,442 --> 00:12:11,452
Number four,

308
00:12:11,452 --> 00:12:13,472
the connection between GPUs.

309
00:12:13,472 --> 00:12:15,744
A server often holds eight GPUs,

310
00:12:15,744 --> 00:12:18,900
joined by a very fast link made by the vendor.

311
00:12:18,900 --> 00:12:21,425
Think of eight cooks in one kitchen,

312
00:12:21,425 --> 00:12:23,445
passing ingredients by hand.

313
00:12:23,794 --> 00:12:25,786
Another server is a second kitchen.

314
00:12:25,786 --> 00:12:27,778
To pass something between kitchens,

315
00:12:27,778 --> 00:12:29,565
you use the building's network.

316
00:12:29,915 --> 00:12:31,429
For an H one hundred,

317
00:12:31,429 --> 00:12:35,790
about four hundred and fifty gigabytes per second per GPU inside the

318
00:12:35,790 --> 00:12:38,334
kitchen. About fifty between kitchens.

319
00:12:38,334 --> 00:12:40,151
Roughly nine times slower,

320
00:12:40,151 --> 00:12:41,302
vendor figures.

321
00:12:41,302 --> 00:12:44,209
That gap shapes everything about big models.

322
00:12:45,459 --> 00:12:47,460
When does the connection matter?

323
00:12:47,460 --> 00:12:49,738
When one model does not fit one card.

324
00:12:49,738 --> 00:12:53,072
A four hundred and five billion model in F P eight needs

325
00:12:53,072 --> 00:12:56,351
about four hundred and five gigabytes of weights alone.

326
00:12:56,351 --> 00:12:58,185
That is eight H one hundreds,

327
00:12:58,185 --> 00:13:00,741
which hold six hundred and forty together.

328
00:13:01,091 --> 00:13:03,604
The model is split across the eight cards,

329
00:13:03,604 --> 00:13:06,008
and they must swap results all the time.

330
00:13:06,008 --> 00:13:09,668
That works well inside one server and badly across the network.

331
00:13:09,668 --> 00:13:11,198
So the bigger the model,

332
00:13:11,198 --> 00:13:13,438
the more the fast connection matters.

333
00:13:14,688 --> 00:13:16,761
Put the four numbers into an order.

334
00:13:16,761 --> 00:13:17,984
First, does it fit,

335
00:13:17,984 --> 00:13:19,951
weights plus chats plus headroom.

336
00:13:19,951 --> 00:13:20,535
If not,

337
00:13:20,535 --> 00:13:21,864
nothing else matters.

338
00:13:22,214 --> 00:13:23,587
Second, memory speed,

339
00:13:23,587 --> 00:13:24,301
for chat.

340
00:13:24,301 --> 00:13:25,344
Third, compute,

341
00:13:25,344 --> 00:13:27,211
for training and long prompts.

342
00:13:27,211 --> 00:13:28,035
And fourth,

343
00:13:28,035 --> 00:13:29,079
the connection,

344
00:13:29,079 --> 00:13:31,110
if you need more than one server.

345
00:13:31,110 --> 00:13:31,934
Fit, speed,

346
00:13:31,934 --> 00:13:33,087
compute, connect.

347
00:13:34,337 --> 00:13:36,238
Let us do this for our project,

348
00:13:36,238 --> 00:13:37,379
ShopNest Copilot.

349
00:13:37,379 --> 00:13:41,343
Say we choose an eight billion parameter model in the smaller format.

350
00:13:41,343 --> 00:13:43,298
The weights are eight gigabytes.

351
00:13:43,648 --> 00:13:46,287
Say we want thirty-two chats open at once,

352
00:13:46,287 --> 00:13:48,353
with eight thousand tokens each,

353
00:13:48,353 --> 00:13:49,959
using the smaller notes.

354
00:13:49,959 --> 00:13:52,369
That is seventeen point two gigabytes.

355
00:13:52,369 --> 00:13:53,517
Add the weights,

356
00:13:53,517 --> 00:13:54,836
add twenty percent,

357
00:13:54,836 --> 00:13:57,361
and the total is about thirty gigabytes.

358
00:13:57,361 --> 00:13:59,139
That fits one mid-size GPU.

359
00:13:59,139 --> 00:14:01,377
Lesson two shows which one to rent.

360
00:14:02,627 --> 00:14:04,546
Four words to carry forward.

361
00:14:04,546 --> 00:14:07,424
Precision: how many bytes each number takes.

362
00:14:07,424 --> 00:14:08,263
K V cache:

363
00:14:08,263 --> 00:14:10,542
the notes kept for each open chat.

364
00:14:10,542 --> 00:14:13,120
Bandwidth: how fast memory can be read.

365
00:14:13,470 --> 00:14:14,258
And H B M,

366
00:14:14,258 --> 00:14:15,721
high bandwidth memory,

367
00:14:15,721 --> 00:14:18,760
the very fast memory built right next to the chip.

368
00:14:18,760 --> 00:14:21,911
You will hear all four in every lesson from here on.

369
00:14:23,161 --> 00:14:24,396
Interview corner.

370
00:14:24,396 --> 00:14:26,749
Asked how to size a GPU for a model,

371
00:14:26,749 --> 00:14:27,984
say: the weights,

372
00:14:27,984 --> 00:14:30,926
which is parameters times bytes per parameter,

373
00:14:30,926 --> 00:14:33,220
plus the notes for every open chat,

374
00:14:33,220 --> 00:14:35,690
plus twenty to forty percent headroom.

375
00:14:36,040 --> 00:14:38,943
Asked why chat speed depends on memory speed,

376
00:14:38,943 --> 00:14:41,965
say: each new token reads all the weights once,

377
00:14:41,965 --> 00:14:45,579
so the time per token is at least the weight size divided

378
00:14:45,579 --> 00:14:47,001
by memory bandwidth.

379
00:14:47,001 --> 00:14:50,438
Batching is how that read is shared across many users.

380
00:14:51,688 --> 00:14:52,475
Your turn.

381
00:14:52,475 --> 00:14:54,950
The zip for this lesson has two scripts.

382
00:14:54,950 --> 00:14:57,199
The first one is the fit calculator.

383
00:14:57,199 --> 00:14:58,661
Change the model size,

384
00:14:58,661 --> 00:15:01,024
the precision and the number of chats,

385
00:15:01,024 --> 00:15:02,767
and see which GPUs survive.

386
00:15:03,117 --> 00:15:05,014
The second is the memory test.

387
00:15:05,014 --> 00:15:08,752
Run it on your own computer and compare your numbers with mine.

388
00:15:08,752 --> 00:15:12,099
One changed number teaches more than an hour of reading.

389
00:15:13,349 --> 00:15:16,083
Recap. A GPU has four numbers that matter:

390
00:15:16,083 --> 00:15:17,034
memory size,

391
00:15:17,034 --> 00:15:18,044
memory speed,

392
00:15:18,044 --> 00:15:19,648
compute and connection.

393
00:15:19,648 --> 00:15:21,966
Weights are parameters times bytes.

394
00:15:21,966 --> 00:15:25,472
The notes for each chat can be bigger than the weights.

395
00:15:25,472 --> 00:15:30,107
Seventy billion in F P eight needed one hundred and eighty-seven gigabytes

396
00:15:30,107 --> 00:15:31,593
for thirty-two chats.

397
00:15:31,943 --> 00:15:34,225
Chat speed is set by memory speed,

398
00:15:34,225 --> 00:15:36,267
and batching shares each read.

399
00:15:36,267 --> 00:15:37,228
On my C P U,

400
00:15:37,228 --> 00:15:41,371
two hundred and fifty-six at once gave about ten times the tokens

401
00:15:41,371 --> 00:15:42,272
per second.

402
00:15:42,272 --> 00:15:43,233
Next lesson:

403
00:15:43,233 --> 00:15:44,614
which chip to rent,

404
00:15:44,614 --> 00:15:45,695
what it costs,

405
00:15:45,695 --> 00:15:48,337
and how spot and autoscaling save money.

406
00:15:49,587 --> 00:15:50,835
In the next lesson:

407
00:15:50,835 --> 00:15:51,920
renting compute.

408
00:15:51,920 --> 00:15:52,733
Which chip,

409
00:15:52,733 --> 00:15:54,089
what it really costs,

410
00:15:54,089 --> 00:15:56,965
and how spot machines and autoscaling save money.

411
00:15:57,315 --> 00:15:58,907
Subscribe so you don't miss it.

412
00:15:58,907 --> 00:16:00,181
See you in the notebook.

